{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1528 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b796ebc64bfa215c7ca15b0877809d4ef3cbf97b59286ccba6ce4798171f3ac7" :edb/db-before-t 1527 :edb/db-after-t 1528 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534840 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:41:04.031Z" 13194139534840 true] [17592186175845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313761 13194139534840 true] [17592186175845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313789 13194139534840 true] [17592186175845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313801 13194139534840 true] [17592186175845 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313808 13194139534840 true] [17592186175883 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313782 13194139534840 true] [17592186175883 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313792 13194139534840 true] [17592186175883 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313815 13194139534840 true] [17592186175883 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313822 13194139534840 true] [17592186175942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313768 13194139534840 true] [17592186175942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313775 13194139534840 true] [17592186175942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313795 13194139534840 true] [17592186175942 1020 #edb/ref 17592186313798 13194139534840 true] [17592186176446 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534840 true] [17592186176446 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = x^{2}y, y(1) = 1$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$." 13194139534840 false] [17592186176446 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" 13194139534840 true] [17592186176446 1145 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={1}^{2}\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={2}^{2}\\cdot 2=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈10.$" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1017 #edb/ref 17592186313762 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1089 "q-117941" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2x,\\,y(2)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1113 #uuid "d3cedab7-42aa-56ef-928f-3cf1df645c6b" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313761 1145 "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=2$ to $x=4$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈10.$" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313763 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313764 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313765 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313766 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1000 #edb/ref 17592186313767 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1041 "selection" 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1104 #edb/ref 17592186313764 13194139534840 true] [17592186313762 1110 #uuid "ed466e7b-ddf1-5f50-9ff2-646c178ee31f" 13194139534840 true]